### How the security goals are met

**Encryption at rest.**  
Each secret value is encrypted with **AES-256-GCM** using a key derived from the master password via **PBKDF2** (100 000 iterations, random 16-byte salt). The stored blob is `IV ‖ ciphertext‖tag` base64-encoded; an eavesreader on the KV store sees only ciphertext whose length reveals only the plaintext length rounded up to the GCM tag boundary.

**Owner isolation.**  
The `(owner, name)` pair is fed into the GCM **authenticated additional data (AAD)** via a length-prefixed encoding (`uint32BE(owner_len) ‖ owner ‖ uint32BE(name_len) ‖ name`). Because GCM binds AAD into the authentication tag, copying another secret's blob onto a different `(owner, name)` key causes decryption to fail—the tag won't match the new AAD.

**Tamper detection.**  
Any modification to the stored blob—bit-flipping the ciphertext, swapping the IV, appending bytes, or overwriting the entry with another secret's data—causes the GCM authentication check to fail. `get` catches the resulting `DOMException` from `crypto.subtle.decrypt` and throws `new Error("Integrity check failed")`.

**Wrong master password.**  
A different password derives a different AES key, so every GCM decryption fails and `get` throws. The `createSecretStore` call itself does not reject (it only rejects if the stored salt is itself corrupt), which satisfies the "either … or …" requirement.

**Storage-key encoding.**  
Owner and name are base64-encoded (of their UTF-8 bytes) and joined with a `\x00` separator, all under the `\x00secret\x00` namespace. Base64 output contains only `[A-Za-z0-9+/=]`, so the `\x00` delimiters are unambiguous even when owner/name contain colons, slashes, newlines, or NUL characters.

